1) Firmware Architecture: 
    Library imports 
    Constants and variables definition (including Pins, wifi, and HTTP configuration)
    Sensor structure 
    Wifi event handler (responsible for connecting and blinking the LED)
    GPIO and memory (queue and flash memory) configuration 
    Wifi disconnection retry logoic 
    Sensor reading logic
    JSON structure (including backend request generations and confirmations)
    Retry stored readings while disconnected (push the saved values in the queue to the API)
    MAIN Loop

2) Pin Mapping: 
      Digital sensor input	GPIO27	Configured as GPIO input with pull-down enabled
      Analog sensor input	GPIO34  	Used for analog voltage reading from 0V to 3.3V
      Status LED	GPIO2	Blinks while connecting, solid when connected, fast blink on error
      Wi-Fi communication	Internal ESP32 radio	Used to send data to backend over HTTP
      Power input	5V to dev board VIN/USB or regulated 3.3V to ESP32 module	12V must not be applied directly to ESP32 pins
      Ground	GND	Common ground required between ESP32, sensors, and power supply



3) Sensor communication type is using I2C
    Analog: ADC voltage = ADC raw value / 4095 × 3.3V  
    The digital sensor is assumed to provide a simple HIGH or LOW output. It is connected to a GPIO input pin and read using gpio_get_level().
    The firmware assumes that all sensor outputs are 3.3V. No 5V signal should be connected directly to ESP32 GPIO or ADC pins. If a sensor outputs 5V, a voltage divider or level shifter must be added.
    The analog sensor is sampled every 5 seconds. The firmware averages the last 5 readings before sending data, meaning one averaged reading is sent every 25 seconds. This helps reduce noise and gives a more stable reading.



4) The PCB is assumed to contain an ESP32 module or ESP32 development-board equivalent, sensor connectors, power regulation, status LED, and programming access.

    Main PCB assumptions:
    
    The ESP32 is powered from a stable 3.3V rail. If the input supply is 12V, a regulator should first convert 12V to 5V or 3.3V
    The analog sensor trace should be kept away from noisy power traces, motors, and switching regulators.
    The ESP32 antenna area should have a proper zone with no copper, traces, batteries, connectors, or metal parts nearby
    Decoupling capacitors should be placed close to the ESP32 power pins and sensor supply pins.
    Programming access should be available through UART pins, EN, BOOT, 3.3V, and GND.
    Test points should be added for 3.3V, GND, analog input, digital input, and reset/debug signals.

